Startup Probe : Usually used for the application that has slow staring problem 

Meaning : "Has the application finished starting properly "

Why this exits: 
Some applications take a long time to start (Java, Spring Boot, DBs, ML apps).
Without this, Kubernetes may think the app is broken too early.

What it does is: 
*Runs only during the startup phase
*Blocks other probes (readiness and liveness)
*Once it succeds -> normal check begins

Simple meaning:  "Don't disturb the app until it fully boots "
Prevents: CrashLoopBackOff during slow startup 

2.Readiness Probe 

Meaning: "Is the application is ready to serve the traffic "

Why it exits: 
Even if the app  is running ,it may not be ready 
*DB not connected 
*cache loading
*config not loaded

what it does:
*Controls traffic routing
*if failed -> Pod is NOT given traffic
*Pod still runs normallly 


Simple meaning: "Don't send users until everything is ready "
It prevent : "Connection failure like Pod is  alive but not serving requests"

3.Liveness Probe

Meaning : "Is the application still healthy and alive ?"

Why it exits: 
*Sometimes app is:
   * Stuck in infinite loop
   * Deadlocked
   * memory leak 
   * not responding 
But container is still "running"


What it does:
 * continuously checks health
 * If fails -> Kubernetes restart the container 



Example: 

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata: 
  name: simpleapp-deployment
spec:
  selector: 
    matchLables: 
      app: simpleapp
  template: 
    metadata:
      lables:
        app: simpleapp
    spec: 
      containers: 
        - name: simpleapp
          image: gowdaamith/app:v1
          ports: 
            - containerPort: 80
          startupProbe:
            httpGet:                      #kubernetes will call the app using http requests 
              path: /                     #it hits http://pod-ip
              port: 80                    # on port 80
              failureThreshold: 30        # allow 30 failur to happen 
              periodSeconds: 10           # gap between each check is 10sec
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: 
              path: /
              port: 80
              initialDelaySeconds: 5       # Wait 5sec after starting the container before checking               
              periodSeconds: 5             # gap between every 5 seconds
          livenesProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /
              port: 80
              initialDeloySeconds: 15      # Wait for 15sec after starting the container before checking
              periodSeconds: 10            # gap betweeen each check




If readiness is not good  the pods will not recive the traffic
If livveness is not good the pods will get replace by another new pod



Kubernetes Probes support 3 types of checks : 
1. httpGet   → HTTP endpoint check
2. tcpSocket → port connectivity check
3. exec      → run a command inside container



httpGet Probe : Used when your app exposes HTTP endpoints (Node.js, Spring Boot, Flask)  

Ex: 

livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 3000
  initialDelaySeconds: 10
  periodSeconds: 10
  timeoutSeconds: 3

Used for : 

*Node.js app
*Java Spring Boot
*Python Flask / FastAPI
*Web servers (Nginx, Express)


tcpSocket Probe:   Used when there is NO HTTP API, only a port is exposed
Example (MongoDB / Redis / PostgreSQL)

readinessProbe:
  tcpSocket:
    port: 27017
  initialDelaySeconds: 5
  periodSeconds: 5


Use for:
*MongoDB
*Redis
*MySQL / PostgreSQL
*Kafka
*RabbitMQ


3.exec Probe (Deep Health check) : Runs commands inside the containers
livenessProbe:
  exec:
    command:
      - mongosh
      - --eval
      - "db.runCommand({ ping: 1 })"
  initialDelaySeconds: 20
  periodSeconds: 10

Meaning: Runs command inside the container and check if it succeeds or not 


✅ Use for:
*DB health validation
*custom app checks
*checking disk, memory, or internal state


What is gRPC?
gRPC is a high performance communication protocol used for service to service communication 

Instead fo HTTP + JSON ,is uses
* HTTP/2  (faster)
* Binary data (Protocol buffer / protobuf)
* Strongly typed API's

gRPC in Kubernetes probes: 
kubernetes also supports health checks

livenessProbe: 
  grpc:
    port: 50051

Meaning: "call gRPC health service on port 50051 and check if service is healthy "

Ex: gRPC service

A backend might expose:
service HealthService {
  rpc Check (HealthRequest) returns (HealthResponse);
}

Kubernetes calls it internally to check health.

>> When to use gRPC probe
Use it when:

your app is built with gRPC
microservices communicate via protobuf
no HTTP endpoints exist

⚠️ When NOT to use it
normal REST APIs → use httpGet
databases → use tcpSocket or exec

